00:10:00.368 --> 00:10:01.877
with #shareyourspot.

00:10:01.877 --> 00:10:03.218
So get on it.

00:10:03.218 --> 00:10:06.475
Now, I'm standing in the
historic Hubbard Hall,

00:10:06.475 --> 00:10:07.798
the original home

00:10:07.798 --> 00:10:10.933
of the nonprofit National
Geographic Society.

00:10:10.933 --> 00:10:14.289
For over 130 years, the
society has been investing

00:10:14.289 --> 00:10:16.961
in bold people and transformative ideas,

00:10:16.961 --> 00:10:19.839
including the two people
we're about to hear from.

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:21.295
Before that, here's more

00:10:21.295 --> 00:10:24.481
about how the National
Geographic Society drives impact

00:10:24.481 --> 00:10:25.686
by identifying

00:10:25.686 --> 00:10:30.401
and funding an international
community of explorers.

00:10:30.401 --> 00:10:32.720
- [Girl] If I was an Explorer.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.300
- [Girl] If I was an Explorer.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:36.586
- [Boy] I would wake up.

00:10:36.586 --> 00:10:37.873
- [Girl] Put on my hijab.

00:10:37.873 --> 00:10:39.616
- [Boy] Put on my gear.

00:10:39.616 --> 00:10:43.334
- [Girl] I would take
my tools to investigate.

00:10:43.334 --> 00:10:46.292
- [Boy] I'd put on my spacesuit.

00:10:46.292 --> 00:10:50.320
- [Girl] Then I'd shoot
off into the stars.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.284
- [Girl] If I was an explorer,

00:10:51.284 --> 00:10:53.298
I wouldn't care about getting dirty.

00:10:53.298 --> 00:10:55.236
- [Boy] I would take my
findings back to the lab-

00:10:55.236 --> 00:10:57.694
- [Girl] To build things,
create new things.

00:10:57.694 --> 00:10:59.343
- [Dominique] To be an explorer is

00:10:59.343 --> 00:11:01.938
all about understanding this world better.

00:11:01.938 --> 00:11:04.716
- [Hannah] To show the world
a different way of seeing.

00:11:04.716 --> 00:11:07.631
- [Arthur] To venture into
unchartered territory.

00:11:07.631 --> 00:11:08.996
My name is Arthur Huang.

00:11:08.996 --> 00:11:10.231
- [Hannah] Hannah Reyes Morales.

00:11:10.231 --> 00:11:12.839
- [Dominique] Dominique
d'Emille Correia Goncalves.

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:16.839
- [All] And this is what
an explorer looks like.

00:11:23.714 --> 00:11:26.297
(upbeat music)

00:11:41.195 --> 00:11:42.328
- Hey, everyone.

00:11:42.328 --> 00:11:44.358
I'm Dr. Rae Wynn-Grant.

00:11:44.358 --> 00:11:45.488
I'm a wildlife ecologist

00:11:45.488 --> 00:11:47.349
and a National Geographic Explorer.

00:11:47.349 --> 00:11:50.105
Thank you for joining me
and National Geographic

00:11:50.105 --> 00:11:52.497
for this Earth Day Eve celebration.

00:11:52.497 --> 00:11:53.641
I love Earth Day.

00:11:53.641 --> 00:11:56.474
I feel like I celebrate it
every single time I go out

00:11:56.474 --> 00:11:57.720
and do my work.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.792
But on April 22nd, I always
love to connect with nature.

00:12:01.792 --> 00:12:03.757
Whether it's a trail or a local park

00:12:03.757 --> 00:12:06.211
or even going to the beach,
connecting with nature

00:12:06.211 --> 00:12:10.413
on April 22nd is my way of
giving back and saying thanks.

00:12:10.413 --> 00:12:13.363
Some work I've been doing
recently that I'm super proud of,

00:12:13.363 --> 00:12:16.976
it's aiming to protect
female animals to make sure

00:12:16.976 --> 00:12:20.148
that they have the healthy,
thriving habitats they need

00:12:20.148 --> 00:12:21.954
in order to give birth.

00:12:21.954 --> 00:12:25.022
Females create the next
generation of wild animals

00:12:25.022 --> 00:12:26.065
for our planet,

00:12:26.065 --> 00:12:29.078
and their health means
the health of the world.

00:12:29.078 --> 00:12:30.430
Where's my spot?

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:31.763
Here is my spot.

00:12:36.669 --> 00:12:37.935
- Hello, everyone.

00:12:37.935 --> 00:12:39.665
My name is Lillygol Sedaghat,

00:12:39.665 --> 00:12:40.951
and I'm a National Geographic Explorer

00:12:40.951 --> 00:12:42.810
here in San Diego, California,

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.839
in my favorite place in the
whole wide world, the slab.

00:12:45.839 --> 00:12:47.621
This place is really important to me.

00:12:47.621 --> 00:12:50.906
It brings together people
and planet and place.

00:12:50.906 --> 00:12:52.211
You've got the blue sky

00:12:52.211 --> 00:12:55.383
and the beautiful golden sun,
big trees, the green grass,

00:12:55.383 --> 00:12:57.463
and this concrete that allows
all of my friends and I

00:12:57.463 --> 00:12:58.799
to come here every weekend

00:12:58.799 --> 00:13:00.854
to be able to practice breakdancing.

00:13:00.854 --> 00:13:02.191
Earth Day is about connectivity

00:13:02.191 --> 00:13:03.396
and understanding that we're connected

00:13:03.396 --> 00:13:05.402
to people and place and planet.

00:13:05.402 --> 00:13:09.128
And this space represents that
to me in its fullest degree.

00:13:09.128 --> 00:13:10.727
So I wanted to share it with you.

00:13:10.727 --> 00:13:12.235
Join me and the Nat Geo family

00:13:12.235 --> 00:13:15.410
for a celebration on Earth Day 2021.

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:16.243
Bye.

00:13:17.567 --> 00:13:20.817
(upbeat hip hop music)

00:14:00.669 --> 00:14:02.136
- Wonderful to hear from Rae,

00:14:02.136 --> 00:14:04.385
and Lillygol, I see that breakdancing.

00:14:04.385 --> 00:14:06.559
Is there a cooler job title

00:14:06.559 --> 00:14:08.809
than National Geographic Explorer?

00:14:08.809 --> 00:14:10.615
Right now, I'm super excited

00:14:10.615 --> 00:14:12.847
to be among the first to step foot

00:14:12.847 --> 00:14:16.265
in the National Geographic
Museum's brand new exhibition,

00:14:16.265 --> 00:14:18.997
Once Upon a Climb, Stories from Everest

00:14:18.997 --> 00:14:20.755
opening later this year.

00:14:20.755 --> 00:14:22.313
The exhibit is the latest

00:14:22.313 --> 00:14:24.215
in National Geographic's long history

00:14:24.215 --> 00:14:25.941
of working on Mount Everest,

00:14:25.941 --> 00:14:28.229
including awarding nearly 200 grants

00:14:28.229 --> 00:14:30.230
for Everest-related projects.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.895
In addition to being the
world's highest mountain,

00:14:32.895 --> 00:14:35.627
Everest is also a massive water tower

00:14:35.627 --> 00:14:37.589
that supplies drinking water

00:14:37.589 --> 00:14:39.434
to over a billion people,

00:14:39.434 --> 00:14:42.539
making its protection
all the more important.

00:14:42.539 --> 00:14:45.805
But now, we travel from Nepal to Sweden

00:14:45.805 --> 00:14:47.753
to link up with Jose Gonzales,

00:14:47.753 --> 00:14:50.652
who'll be performing his
brand new track "Visions"

00:14:50.652 --> 00:14:53.772
off his forthcoming album, "Local Valley."

00:14:53.772 --> 00:14:55.529
Then we'll catch a performance from one

00:14:55.529 --> 00:14:58.011
of the greatest artists
in world music today,

00:14:58.011 --> 00:15:00.511
the legendary Angelique Kidjo.

00:15:04.799 --> 00:15:07.489
- Hi, I'm Jose Gonzalez.

00:15:07.489 --> 00:15:12.441
I'm happy to be here with
you on this Earth Day Eve.

00:15:12.441 --> 00:15:13.740
I'm excited as a longtime fan

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.907
of National Geographic and
as a fan of our planet.

00:15:19.733 --> 00:15:21.855
We're filming this at Saro Vasterskog

00:15:21.855 --> 00:15:26.195
near my hometown Gothenburg
on the west coast of Sweden.

00:15:26.195 --> 00:15:29.069
Since I was a kid, we
used to cycle to the ocean

00:15:29.069 --> 00:15:31.524
to hang out with friends and family.

00:15:31.524 --> 00:15:35.039
And I love it by the archipelago
with its granite rocks,

00:15:35.039 --> 00:15:38.206
oaks and pine trees just by the water.

00:15:42.197 --> 00:15:43.809
For the last couple of
years I've been writing

00:15:43.809 --> 00:15:46.347
and recording close to
nature, taking walks

00:15:46.347 --> 00:15:50.635
while listening to sketches
of my half-finished songs.

00:15:50.635 --> 00:15:52.077
I have to say the forests and water

00:15:52.077 --> 00:15:54.494
give me perspective and calm.

00:15:55.731 --> 00:15:57.721
Suddenly I can pause
my ruminating thoughts

00:15:57.721 --> 00:15:59.138
and feel at ease.

00:16:03.623 --> 00:16:06.287
The song I'm going to
play is called "Visions."

00:16:06.287 --> 00:16:09.848
It's a song for our humanity
and the Anthropocene.

00:16:09.848 --> 00:16:13.085
We are the apes that are starting
to understand the universe

00:16:13.085 --> 00:16:15.283
and our place in it.

00:16:15.283 --> 00:16:16.700
We are changing our surroundings

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:18.833
at an exceedingly rapid pace,

00:16:18.833 --> 00:16:22.029
so much that we're becoming
stewards of our planet,

00:16:22.029 --> 00:16:24.196
whether we like it or not.

00:16:25.984 --> 00:16:28.087
So we need visions,

00:16:28.087 --> 00:16:30.363
visions of the future, collective visions

00:16:30.363 --> 00:16:32.377
that are wise enough
to learn from our past

00:16:32.377 --> 00:16:34.575
and from the present,

00:16:34.575 --> 00:16:38.492
envisioning a future
where we all can flourish.

