00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:32.120
- Next up, the Berkshires,
an epic performance

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:34.721
from the incomparable Yo-Yo Ma awaits.

00:24:34.721 --> 00:24:36.682
He is also featured in the May issue

00:24:36.682 --> 00:24:40.080
of "National Geographic"
magazine now on newsstands.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:43.068
But first, we're honored
to share a special message

00:24:43.068 --> 00:24:46.485
from the ever-inspiring Dr. Jane Goodall.

00:24:47.711 --> 00:24:52.125
- Hello, this is Jane
Goodall, and it's Earth Day.

00:24:52.125 --> 00:24:55.141
So thank you for joining me on Earth Day.

00:24:55.141 --> 00:24:58.885
It's a day when we should
be celebrating the wonder

00:24:58.885 --> 00:25:02.213
of this amazing planet on which we live.

00:25:02.213 --> 00:25:07.213
It's our only home, and we
still have so much to discover.

00:25:07.645 --> 00:25:11.751
But the tragedy is that we've
been disrespecting nature

00:25:11.751 --> 00:25:14.668
and this is causing climate change.

00:25:15.914 --> 00:25:18.514
It's causing the loss of species,

00:25:18.514 --> 00:25:20.389
the loss of biodiversities.

00:25:20.389 --> 00:25:23.377
So we're in the sixth great extinction.

00:25:23.377 --> 00:25:26.820
And so today on Earth
Day, we need to reflect

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:28.900
on the harm that we've done

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:32.136
and think about ways in
which we can work together

00:25:32.136 --> 00:25:35.062
to heal that harm before it's too late.

00:25:35.062 --> 00:25:37.667
And you know, just as an aside,

00:25:37.667 --> 00:25:40.338
it was our disrespect
of nature and animals

00:25:40.338 --> 00:25:42.513
that caused this terrible pandemic

00:25:42.513 --> 00:25:45.987
that's led to so much
suffering around the world.

00:25:45.987 --> 00:25:50.414
And you know, we created
conditions with trafficking animals

00:25:50.414 --> 00:25:52.533
around the world, killing
them, eating them,

00:25:52.533 --> 00:25:55.575
wildlife markets, factory farms.

00:25:55.575 --> 00:25:56.907
In all these things,

00:25:56.907 --> 00:26:00.074
we create conditions where a pathogen,

00:26:01.101 --> 00:26:04.873
like a virus can jump
from an animal to a person

00:26:04.873 --> 00:26:07.449
and sometimes create a new disease,

00:26:07.449 --> 00:26:10.357
as was the case with COVID-19.

00:26:10.357 --> 00:26:12.701
Bad luck for us that was very contagious

00:26:12.701 --> 00:26:15.561
and just went racing around the world.

00:26:15.561 --> 00:26:16.975
And we'll get through that,

00:26:16.975 --> 00:26:20.173
but then we're faced
with two real threats,

00:26:20.173 --> 00:26:22.966
climate change and loss of biodiversity.

00:26:22.966 --> 00:26:26.932
And that also is caused by our disrespect

00:26:26.932 --> 00:26:30.932
for both of these, by our
disrespect for nature.

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:34.483
So as I say on this Earth Day,

00:26:34.483 --> 00:26:38.101
let's get together and
start thinking of ways

00:26:38.101 --> 00:26:41.835
in which we can heal some of this harm.

00:26:41.835 --> 00:26:43.471
What can you do?

00:26:43.471 --> 00:26:45.403
What can I do?

00:26:45.403 --> 00:26:48.123
I think first of all, we can make lists

00:26:48.123 --> 00:26:53.123
of what we as individuals
can do to save the planet.

00:26:53.153 --> 00:26:56.052
And that's something you
can all do, sit down,

00:26:56.052 --> 00:26:58.538
discuss it with your
friends, your parents,

00:26:58.538 --> 00:27:02.205
and make a list, but
not just for Earth Day.

00:27:03.775 --> 00:27:06.767
We should look at this
list every single day,

00:27:06.767 --> 00:27:10.473
because that's what it's going
to take to save the planet.

00:27:10.473 --> 00:27:14.109
So the message really
is Earth Day every day.

00:27:14.109 --> 00:27:16.338
Earth Day for planet Earth,

00:27:16.338 --> 00:27:19.389
this beautiful planet that nurtures us,

00:27:19.389 --> 00:27:22.367
this environment of which we are part

00:27:22.367 --> 00:27:24.461
and on which we depend,

00:27:24.461 --> 00:27:27.009
and we mustn't go on destroying it.

00:27:27.009 --> 00:27:29.345
Partly because it's
beautiful in its own right,

00:27:29.345 --> 00:27:31.195
partly because it's destroying

00:27:31.195 --> 00:27:33.987
our great-grandchildren's future,

00:27:33.987 --> 00:27:35.887
if we don't start healing it.

00:27:35.887 --> 00:27:39.445
We're really using up some
natural resources faster

00:27:39.445 --> 00:27:42.141
than nature can replenish them,

00:27:42.141 --> 00:27:44.259
and as species become extinct,

00:27:44.259 --> 00:27:46.259
so I like to think of it

00:27:47.275 --> 00:27:51.016
as tearing holes in this
beautiful tapestry of life.

00:27:51.016 --> 00:27:54.513
And as more and more
species become extinct,

00:27:54.513 --> 00:27:56.465
so this tapestry becomes more

00:27:56.465 --> 00:28:01.465
and more tattered and a
whole ecosystem can collapse.

00:28:01.575 --> 00:28:05.325
So think of what you
can do as an individual.

00:28:06.642 --> 00:28:10.201
I try to think every day
of my ecological footprint.

00:28:10.201 --> 00:28:13.233
I try to turn off lights
and not waste water,

00:28:13.233 --> 00:28:18.004
and well, in the pandemic,
I haven't been able to fly

00:28:18.004 --> 00:28:20.205
or I haven't even driven anywhere.

00:28:20.205 --> 00:28:23.623
So I think my footprint
has been especially light.

00:28:23.623 --> 00:28:28.300
I'm not eating meat and trying
to keep away from dairy.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:32.123
I've been pretty well vegan
while I've been at home

00:28:32.123 --> 00:28:34.841
and sending out messages
all over the world

00:28:34.841 --> 00:28:36.905
on Zooms and podcasts,

00:28:36.905 --> 00:28:41.053
so that we encourage more and more people

00:28:41.053 --> 00:28:45.200
to lead lives that are
friendly towards nature.

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:48.206
And that's what you can do.

00:28:48.206 --> 00:28:50.596
And our Roots &amp; Shoots program,

00:28:50.596 --> 00:28:53.960
which is for young people
from preschool, university

00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:57.406
and everything in between
and even some adults now,

00:28:57.406 --> 00:28:59.333
these are exactly the kind of things

00:28:59.333 --> 00:29:01.541
that they and other youth programs

00:29:01.541 --> 00:29:03.989
with the same mission are doing.

00:29:03.989 --> 00:29:06.457
So think about all of this on Earth Day.

00:29:06.457 --> 00:29:11.023
Do your bit and together
we can save the planet.

00:29:11.023 --> 00:29:12.606
Thank you. Goodbye.

00:29:15.072 --> 00:29:19.575
- I'm so happy to join you
from the grounds of Tanglewood.

00:29:19.575 --> 00:29:22.211
These Berkshire Hills
in western Massachusetts

00:29:22.211 --> 00:29:25.809
have been a spiritual home for
many, a source of inspiration

00:29:25.809 --> 00:29:28.012
for makers of music and poetry,

00:29:28.012 --> 00:29:31.972
for builders of homes and
communities for millennia.

00:29:31.972 --> 00:29:33.588
Look to the shores of the lake

00:29:33.588 --> 00:29:36.749
in the distance, Lake Mackinac.

00:29:36.749 --> 00:29:39.567
Mackinac, the home of the Mohicans,

00:29:39.567 --> 00:29:41.361
the indigenous people of this place

00:29:41.361 --> 00:29:43.599
who live now in upper Wisconsin

00:29:43.599 --> 00:29:46.573
and are known as the
Stockbridge-Munsee Band

00:29:46.573 --> 00:29:48.667
of Mohican Indians are the people

00:29:48.667 --> 00:29:51.853
for whom the waters are never still.

00:29:51.853 --> 00:29:54.869
They used to share music
on the shores of the lake,

00:29:54.869 --> 00:29:58.991
and they still cherish this
land as hallowed ground.

00:29:58.991 --> 00:30:03.265
Today, by combining the
human enterprise of music

00:30:03.265 --> 00:30:05.409
with the majesty of nature,

00:30:05.409 --> 00:30:07.502
we continue a sacred practice.

00:30:07.502 --> 00:30:10.143
Through the ephemeral nature of music,

00:30:10.143 --> 00:30:14.057
we are reminded that we are
but temporary custodians

00:30:14.057 --> 00:30:15.029
of this land.

00:30:15.029 --> 00:30:19.139
And it is our duty to cultivate
the land and ourselves

00:30:19.139 --> 00:30:21.937
to find balance between humans and nature

00:30:21.937 --> 00:30:25.199
as this place's indigenous
people teach us,

00:30:25.199 --> 00:30:29.321
in order that our species
can survive and thrive.

00:30:29.321 --> 00:30:33.404
So with gratitude, here
is this offering of music

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:37.712
for our Earth Day celebration.

00:30:37.712 --> 00:30:40.795
(bright cello music)

00:35:04.269 --> 00:35:06.714
- Such a moving performance from Yo-Yo Ma.

00:35:06.714 --> 00:35:10.003
You can learn more about him
in Nat Geo's new book "Spark"

00:35:10.003 --> 00:35:13.813
coming out April 27th from
bestselling author Claudia Kalb.

00:35:13.813 --> 00:35:16.063
And does it get any better than Jane?

