00:40:43.059 --> 00:40:44.311
- I'm Lucy Hawkes.

00:40:44.311 --> 00:40:47.227
I'm a marine biologist and a
National Geographic Explorer,

00:40:47.227 --> 00:40:50.298
and I'm here in Devon on
the south coast of England.

00:40:50.298 --> 00:40:53.325
I'm passionate about
protecting the Earth around me,

00:40:53.325 --> 00:40:55.509
because I study some of
the most amazing animals

00:40:55.509 --> 00:40:57.026
that we share it with.

00:40:57.026 --> 00:40:58.806
I study animal migration,

00:40:58.806 --> 00:41:01.458
and I use tracking tags to
follow the incredible movements

00:41:01.458 --> 00:41:03.191
of migratory species.

00:41:03.191 --> 00:41:05.441
Because of my work, I've
been able to find out

00:41:05.441 --> 00:41:07.289
that birds can make flights

00:41:07.289 --> 00:41:09.651
of more than 1000 miles at a time

00:41:09.651 --> 00:41:13.227
without needing to stop to
eat, to drink or to sleep.

00:41:13.227 --> 00:41:15.801
Or that sea turtles in our
oceans can stay under water

00:41:15.801 --> 00:41:18.313
for more than seven hours at a time.

00:41:18.313 --> 00:41:22.525
And currently, I'm studying
a small angry white seabird

00:41:22.525 --> 00:41:24.306
called the Arctic tern.

00:41:24.306 --> 00:41:27.437
These birds weigh about
the same as a bar of soap,

00:41:27.437 --> 00:41:29.541
but they can make a journey every year

00:41:29.541 --> 00:41:32.349
from the Arctic to
Antarctica and back again

00:41:32.349 --> 00:41:35.188
clocking enough distance
over the course of their life

00:41:35.188 --> 00:41:38.096
to make six return trips
to the moon and back.

00:41:38.096 --> 00:41:39.255
We're tracking them

00:41:39.255 --> 00:41:41.543
using tiny little tracking tags like this,

00:41:41.543 --> 00:41:43.858
and I can't tell you
what a privilege it is

00:41:43.858 --> 00:41:45.561
as a scientist to be allowed

00:41:45.561 --> 00:41:48.330
to hold this amazing bird in my hand,

00:41:48.330 --> 00:41:50.278
to look at this tiny little creature

00:41:50.278 --> 00:41:53.879
that sees the world at a planetary scale.

00:41:53.879 --> 00:41:56.518
And the more I study these
animals, the more passionate I am

00:41:56.518 --> 00:42:00.121
about protecting the Earth
around me and around you too.

00:42:00.121 --> 00:42:03.029
Because every spring and
fall the skies above you

00:42:03.029 --> 00:42:04.645
and the oceans around you are filled

00:42:04.645 --> 00:42:06.579
with incredible migratory species

00:42:06.579 --> 00:42:08.337
no matter where you live on Earth.

00:42:08.337 --> 00:42:11.609
And I consider myself really
lucky to share the planet

00:42:11.609 --> 00:42:12.871
with these animals.

00:42:12.871 --> 00:42:15.431
So thank you for joining me
and the National Geographic

00:42:15.431 --> 00:42:18.264
on this Earth Day Eve celebration.

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:21.503
- Hello there, I'm Valerie June.

00:42:21.503 --> 00:42:23.402
I recorded my performance

00:42:23.402 --> 00:42:27.032
for National Geographic at Prospect Park,

00:42:27.032 --> 00:42:31.410
and I recorded a song
called "Home Inside."

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:33.671
It's a song to remind all of us

00:42:33.671 --> 00:42:35.972
that Earth is a school,

00:42:35.972 --> 00:42:37.598
and how we treat the Earth

00:42:37.598 --> 00:42:40.722
and how we respect each other is super,

00:42:40.722 --> 00:42:44.449
super important for the
healing of our planet.

00:42:44.449 --> 00:42:46.140
As I wrote in my book,

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:50.554
Mother Earth is continuously
trying to communicate with us.

00:42:50.554 --> 00:42:53.154
The environment is in
a state of emergency.

00:42:53.154 --> 00:42:55.656
We're facing a climate crisis.

00:42:55.656 --> 00:42:58.256
But miraculous things can happen

00:42:58.256 --> 00:43:00.780
when we are living mindfully.

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:03.559
The smallest steps to respect the Earth

00:43:03.559 --> 00:43:06.616
could positively shift the planet faster

00:43:06.616 --> 00:43:10.216
than we could ever
dream would be possible.

00:43:10.216 --> 00:43:12.205
I do believe that each citizen

00:43:12.205 --> 00:43:17.042
of the planet has a responsibility
to respect the Earth,

00:43:17.042 --> 00:43:18.422
to treat it kind,

00:43:18.422 --> 00:43:22.789
and in doing so, love
and respect each other.

00:43:22.789 --> 00:43:24.039
Enjoy the song.

