00:45:41.159 --> 00:45:45.177
Next up, we've got National
Geographic Explorer

00:45:45.177 --> 00:45:47.140
and photographer Brian Skerry

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:49.972
giving us a sneak peek at
"Secrets of the Whales,"

00:45:49.972 --> 00:45:51.768
the epic four-part series

00:45:51.768 --> 00:45:55.101
premiering on Disney+ on Earth Day.

00:45:56.122 --> 00:45:57.578
- Hi, I'm Brian Skerry,

00:45:57.578 --> 00:46:00.074
National Geographic
photographer and producer.

00:46:00.074 --> 00:46:02.168
And thank you for joining me and Nat Geo

00:46:02.168 --> 00:46:04.609
for this Earth Day Eve celebration.

00:46:04.609 --> 00:46:05.936
You know, throughout all the years

00:46:05.936 --> 00:46:07.811
that I've been exploring
the world's oceans,

00:46:07.811 --> 00:46:09.958
I've seen so many beautiful things,

00:46:09.958 --> 00:46:13.169
but I often see some pretty
bad things out there too.

00:46:13.169 --> 00:46:15.121
Still, I remain hopeful

00:46:15.121 --> 00:46:18.017
because I know the ocean has
the ability to heal itself

00:46:18.017 --> 00:46:19.749
if we simply protect it.

00:46:19.749 --> 00:46:21.805
I first learned about this while working

00:46:21.805 --> 00:46:24.454
on a story in New Zealand
about marine-protected areas

00:46:24.454 --> 00:46:27.745
and saw underwater
ecosystems that had rebounded

00:46:27.745 --> 00:46:30.464
and were thriving because of conservation.

00:46:30.464 --> 00:46:33.112
So the place that makes
me feel most hopeful

00:46:33.112 --> 00:46:34.423
about the future of the planet

00:46:34.423 --> 00:46:36.701
is the Poor Knights Island in New Zealand,

00:46:36.701 --> 00:46:40.237
because it was there that I
first learned what was possible.

00:46:40.237 --> 00:46:42.275
I think every day should be Earth Day,

00:46:42.275 --> 00:46:44.146
because I've seen time and time again

00:46:44.146 --> 00:46:46.765
just how extraordinary our planet is.

00:46:46.765 --> 00:46:48.976
I remain passionate about our planet,

00:46:48.976 --> 00:46:51.199
because whether you travel
to the ends of the Earth

00:46:51.199 --> 00:46:54.263
or into your own backyard,
discoveries can be made.

00:46:54.263 --> 00:46:56.513
And while Earth Day is
always a special day,

00:46:56.513 --> 00:46:58.843
this year there's a special
reason to celebrate,

00:46:58.843 --> 00:47:01.764
my three-year project
about whales culminates

00:47:01.764 --> 00:47:03.179
with the Earth Day premiere

00:47:03.179 --> 00:47:06.653
of the Disney+ original series
"Secrets of the Whales."

00:47:06.653 --> 00:47:09.447
It's also the subject of my
new National Geographic book

00:47:09.447 --> 00:47:11.774
of the same name, as well as a cover story

00:47:11.774 --> 00:47:14.689
in the May issue of "National
Geographic" magazine.

00:47:14.689 --> 00:47:16.218
I'm excited to share a clip

00:47:16.218 --> 00:47:17.521
from the series with all of you

00:47:17.521 --> 00:47:19.563
and hope you'll check it out on Disney+

00:47:19.563 --> 00:47:21.838
starting at midnight.

